msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus supports continuous testing, where tests run immediately after code "
"changes have been saved. This allows you to\n"
"get instant feedback on your code changes. Quarkus detects which tests cover "
"which code, and uses this information to\n"
"only run the relevant tests when code is changed."
msgstr ""

msgid "Solution"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Start the xref:getting-started.adoc[Getting Started] application (or any "
"other application) using:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus will start in development mode as normal, but down the bottom of the "
"screen you should see the following:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Press `r` and the tests will start running. You should see the status change "
"down the bottom of the screen as they\n"
"are running, and it should finish with:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Now you can start making changes to your application. Go into the "
"`GreetingResource` and change the hello endpoint to\n"
"return `\"hello world\"`, and save the file. Quarkus should immediately re-"
"run the test, and you should get output similar\n"
"to the following:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Change it back and the tests will run again."
msgstr ""

msgid "Controlling Continuous Testing"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"There are various hotkeys you can use to control continuous testing. "
"Pressing `h` will display the following list\n"
"of commands:"
msgstr ""

msgid "These are explained below:"
msgstr ""

msgid "[r] - Re-run all tests"
msgstr ""

msgid "This will re-run every test"
msgstr ""

msgid "[f] - Re-run failed tests"
msgstr ""

msgid "This will re-run every failing test"
msgstr ""

msgid "[b] - Toggle 'broken only' mode, where only failing tests are run"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Broken only mode will only run tests that have previously failed, even if "
"other tests would normally be affected by a code\n"
"change. This can be useful if you are modifying code that is used by lots of "
"tests, but you only want to focus on debugging\n"
"the failing one."
msgstr ""

msgid "[v] - Print failures from the last test run"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Prints the failures to the console again, this can be useful if there has "
"been lots of console output since the last run."
msgstr ""

msgid "[p] - Pause tests"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Temporarily stops running tests. This can be useful if you are making lots "
"of changes, and don't want feedback until they\n"
"are all done."
msgstr ""

msgid "[o] - Toggle test output"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"By default test output is filtered and not displayed on the console, so that "
"test output and dev mode output is not\n"
"interleaved. Enabling test output will print output to the console when "
"tests are run. Even when output is disabled\n"
"the filtered output is saved and can be viewed in the Dev UI."
msgstr ""

msgid "[i] - Toggle instrumentation based reload"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is not directly related to testing, but allows you to toggle "
"instrumentation based reload. This will allow live reload\n"
"to avoid a restart if a change does not affect the structure of a class, "
"which gives a faster reload and allows you to keep\n"
"state."
msgstr ""

msgid "[l] - Toggle live reload"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is not directly related to testing, but allows you to turn live reload "
"on and off."
msgstr ""

msgid "[s] - Force restart"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This will force a scan for changed files, and will perform a live reload "
"with and changes. Note that even if there are no\n"
"changes the application will still restart. This will still work even if "
"live reload is disabled."
msgstr ""

msgid "Continuous Testing Without Dev Mode"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It is possible to run continuous testing without starting dev mode. This can "
"be useful if dev mode will interfere with\n"
"your tests (e.g. running wiremock on the same port), or if you only want to "
"develop using tests. To start continuous testing\n"
"mode, run `mvn quarkus:test` or `gradle quarkusTest`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Selecting Tests to Run"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The configuration properties `quarkus.test.include-pattern` and `quarkus."
"test.exclude-pattern` can be used to select which tests to run.\n"
"They are regular expressions matched against the fully qualified class name "
"of the test class.\n"
"If `include-patterns` is configured, `exclude-patterns` is ignored."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Alternatively, an approach native to the build system may be used.\n"
"In Maven, that is the `-Dtest=\\...` system property, while in Gradle, it is "
"the `--tests \\...` command line option.\n"
"These options, when used with `maven quarkus:[dev|test]` or `gradle "
"quarkus[Dev|Test]`, work just like they work with `mvn test` or `gradle "
"test`, except that they filter the set of tests executed during continuous "
"testing.\n"
"When these options are used, the `quarkus.test.[include|exclude]-pattern` "
"configuration is ignored."
msgstr ""

msgid "Maven"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `-Dtest=\\...` system property selects given test(s) for continuous "
"testing.\n"
"The format of this configuration property is the same as the Maven Surefire "
"`-Dtest=\\...` https://maven.apache.org/surefire/maven-surefire-plugin/test-"
"mojo.html#test[format].\n"
"Specifically: it is a comma (`,`) separated list of globs of class file "
"paths and/or method names.\n"
"Each glob can potentially be prefixed with an exclamation mark (`!`), which "
"makes it an exclusion filter instead of an inclusion filter.\n"
"Exclusions have higher priority than inclusions.\n"
"The class file path glob is separated from the method name glob by the hash "
"sign (`#`) and multiple method name globs may be present, separated by the "
"plus sign (`+`)."
msgstr ""

msgid "For example:"
msgstr ""

msgid "`Basic*`: all classes starting with `Basic`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`???Test`: all classes named with 3 arbitrary characters followed by `Test`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`!Unstable*`: all classes except classes starting with `Unstable`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`pkg/**/Ci*leTest`: all classes in the package `pkg` and subpackages, "
"starting with `Ci` and ending with `leTest`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`*Test#test*One+testTwo?????`: all classes ending with `Test`, and in them, "
"only methods starting with `test` and ending with `One`, or starting with "
"`testTwo` and followed by 5 arbitrary characters"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`#fast*+slowTest`: all classes, and in them, only methods starting with "
"`fast` or methods named `slowTest`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Note that the syntax `%regex[\\...]` and `%ant[\\...]` is _NOT_ supported."
msgstr ""

msgid "Gradle"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `--tests \\...` command line option selects given test(s) for continuous "
"testing.\n"
"The format is the same as the `gradle test --tests \\...` https://docs."
"gradle.org/current/userguide/java_testing.html#test_filtering[format].\n"
"Specifically: the option can be passed multiple times, and each item is a "
"simple pattern for the test class name and optionally a method name.\n"
"When the pattern starts with an upper case letter, it matches a simple name "
"of the class; otherwise, it matches a fully qualified name of the class.\n"
"After the class name, separated by a period (`.`), a method name pattern may "
"be included.\n"
"The only wildcard character supported is `\\*`, which matches an arbitrary "
"number of characters.\n"
"Note that `*` is based purely on text, it doesn't pay extra attention to "
"package delimiters."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`com.example.Basic*`: all classes in package `com.example` starting with "
"`Basic`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`MyTest*`: all classes whose simple name starts with `MyTest`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`\\*.pkg.Test*`: all classes in package `pkg` (regardless of the parent "
"packages) starting with `Test`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`MyTest.test*`: all classes whose simple name is `MyTest`, and in them, only "
"methods starting with `test`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`com.example.IntegTest.fast*`: the class `com.example.IntegTest`, and in it, "
"only methods starting with `fast`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Multi-module Projects"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Note that continuous testing supports multi-module projects, so tests in "
"modules other than the application can still\n"
"be run when files are changed. The modules that are run can be controlled "
"using config as listed below."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is enabled by default, and can be disabled via `quarkus.test.only-test-"
"application-module=true`."
msgstr ""

